<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Work song · c. 1900 · Sub-Saharan Africa</p><h1 class="sg-title">Shosholoza</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A work song of miners who rode the train from Zimbabwe to South Africa's mines; its name means &quot;go forward&quot;.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Ndebele and Zulu mine workers of southern Africa</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Northern Ndebele, Zulu</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Zimbabwe and the mines of South Africa</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>not known (traditional; featured in the musical King Kong, 1959)</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Work song</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional, anonymous)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shosholoza">Shosholoza</a>&quot; is a traditional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguni">Nguni</a> work song of the mine workers of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, in a mix of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndebele-kingdom">Ndebele</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> words into which singers bring other South African languages. It was first sung by groups of Ndebele men who travelled by steam train from their homes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a>, then Rhodesia, to work in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>'s diamond and gold mines; the Ndebele live mainly in Zimbabwe, near its border with South Africa, and the song is Zimbabwean in origin. Some hold that it describes the journey to the mines, others the journey home.</p><p>The song belonged to men doing hard, rhythmical manual labour. It was sung in call and response, one man singing a line and the group answering by copying him, and miners sang it in time with the swing of their axes as they dug. According to the researchers Booth and Nauright, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> workers later took it up to keep the rhythm of group tasks and to ease boredom and strain. It expressed the hardship and heartache of work in the mines, and its title word, meaning &quot;go forward&quot; or &quot;make way for the next man&quot; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndebele-kingdom">Ndebele</a>, served as encouragement and a sign of solidarity among the workers. The repeated &quot;sho sho&quot; imitates the sound of the steam train, the stimela.</p><p>Prisoners also sang it, dividing the parts between rows. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Nelson Mandela</a> described singing &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shosholoza">Shosholoza</a>&quot; while he worked during his imprisonment on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robben-island">Robben Island</a>, calling it a song that compares the struggle against apartheid to the motion of an oncoming train, and saying that the singing made the work lighter.</p><p>The song was featured in the musical King Kong (1959), whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> production followed in 1961, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pete-seeger">Pete Seeger</a> recorded it on his album <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/we-shall-overcome">We Shall Overcome</a>. It became a standard of gumboot dance bands and a campfire song of South African scouts. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> won the 1995 Rugby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifa-world-cup">World Cup</a> it became a favourite at sporting events; a recording sung by the radio host Dan Moyane, produced by Stuart Lee, was made and released in five days for the tournament's first game and went gold. The South African football team sang it as they came onto the field to open the 2010 FIFA World Cup.</p><p>The song is now so widely sung that it is often called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>'s second national anthem, used at sporting and national events to tell players they are not alone. Its meaning has widened to support for any struggle: climate activists made it the centrepiece of their Occupy COP17 rally on 9 December 2011, the last day of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> climate negotiations, calling on negotiators to &quot;Stand With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>&quot;. The first African challengers for the America's Cup, Team <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shosholoza">Shosholoza</a>, and the long-distance passenger train service Shosholoza Meyl took their names from it.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song of men at hard labour far from home, carried by the image of the steam train that brought them to the mines. Sung in call and response, it urges the group forward together and sets the rhythm of the work, so that it voices both the weariness of the labour and the strength of solidarity. Later singers heard in it a call to keep going through any struggle.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Northern Ndebele</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Northern Ndebele</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nd">Shosholoza</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Shosholoza</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nd">Shosholoza</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Shosholoza</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nd">Kulezo ntaba</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>On those mountains</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nd">Stimela siphume South Africa</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The train that comes from South Africa</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nd">Shosholoza</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Shosholoza</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nd">Kulezo ntaba</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>On those mountains</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nd">Stimela siphume South Africa</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The train that comes from South Africa</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nd">Wen' uyabaleka</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You are running</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nd">Kulezo ntaba</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>On those mountains</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nd">Stimela siphume South Africa</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The train that comes from South Africa</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Traditional Nguni work song (one version among several); as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shosholoza" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (4)</summary><ul><li>Shosholoza is left as it stands: it is a call to push on, move forward together (from a verb for sliding or pushing forward), often heard as imitating the sound of the steam train; it has no single English word.</li><li>line 1.3 etc.: 'Kulezo ntaba' is 'on (from, in) those mountains'; the locative can mean any of these.</li><li>line 1.4 etc.: 'Stimela' is the train (from English 'steam'); 'siphume' is read as 'that comes / came out of'. Other versions of the song name Rhodesia or Zimbabwe instead of South Africa: it was sung by migrant workers travelling to and from the mines.</li><li>line 1.8: 'Wen' uyabaleka' is 'You are running (away)'; 'ukubaleka' can mean to run or to flee.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
